# Level-5 Explanation: Why the Pack Is Structured This Way

## Preserve

State the capability, source truth, compatibility surface, and user consequence that must survive change.

## Fence

Identify Chesterton fences and the failure modes they prevent. A simpler-looking implementation is not equivalent unless it preserves the same boundaries.

## Calculus

Define the system's objects and lawful morphisms: observation, admission/refusal, construction, verification, authority transition, receipt, replay, and standing.

## Exclusions

State what the pack deliberately does not own, generate, execute, or authorize.

## Falsifiers

Name concrete observations that refute the claimed standing or correspondence.

## Extensions

Describe safe extension points and the invariants that new profiles/modules must preserve.

## Authority model

Keep SELECT, CONSTRUCT, and DO separate. Presentation, generated text, planners, proofs, semantic derivations, and hooks cannot grant consequential authority by themselves.

## Receipt and replay model

Explain how identity, admitted source, authority, consequence, and replay are bound so standing can be independently re-established.

## Composition model

Explain which facts are canonical, which capabilities are shared kernels, which are profiles, and how composition avoids duplicated semantic authority.

## Operationalization

Map the explanation back to the real parse -> admit/refuse -> construct -> verify -> receipt -> replay path and the exact-subject execution courts that establish standing.
